A(('h1', 'What You Will Learn in This Study'))
A(('p', 'By the end of this study, you should be able to:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Trace the turns of address in Romans 1:18–3:20 (“men,” “O man,” “thou art called a Jew,” “both Jews and Gentiles”) and note, for each, whom Paul charges and with what.',
    'Read Romans 1:18–32 in order and mark each verb of revealing and each “gave them up” or “gave them over,” recording what the passage states and what it leaves unsaid.',
    'Read Romans 2:1–16 clause by clause, including the sentences on reward in verses 6–10, and observe where the passage speaks of the law, of nature and of “their conscience also bearing witness.”',
    'Follow Romans 2:17–3:8 and record what is said of “a Jew,” of circumcision and of the “oracles of God” without adding an identity label the text does not give.',
    'Set the sentences of Romans 3:9–18 beside the verse that introduces them and the verses that close the passage (3:19–20), and note where the charge stops.',
    'Test two common readings of the passage against its own words, sorting what the text states from what readers add.',
]))
A(('call', 'Study Goal',
   'To read Romans 1:18–3:20 in the order Paul wrote it, and to set down whom each part addresses, what it charges, what witness it names, and what it leaves unsaid, so that every reading of the passage rests on what it states and goes no further.'))
A(('nav', 'Study Outline'))
A(('num', [
    'The Setting: one letter and its turns of address.',
    'Romans 1:18–32: what is revealed and what God gave up.',
    'Romans 2:1–16: the one who judges and the Gentiles who have not the law.',
    'Romans 2:17–3:8: “a Jew,” circumcision and the oracles of God.',
    'Romans 3:9–20: “all under sin” and the written sentences.',
    'Conscience in Paul’s letters, named one at a time.',
    'The Charge Side by Side and Two Readings Tested: one table and two readings set against the words.',
    'Conclusion, Study Summary, Key Distinctions, Scripture-Tracing, Review, Teaching Outline, Guided Further Study and Final Synthesis.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Introduction'))
A(('p', 'Romans 1–3 is often quoted in short pieces: “all have sinned,” “there is none righteous,” “every mouth may be stopped.” The pieces are easy to carry away, and the order in which Paul wrote them is easy to lose. The passage moves from “all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men” (1:18) to “O man, whosoever thou art that judgest” (2:1), to one who “art called a Jew” (2:17), to a charge that “they are all under sin” (3:9), and it ends where the law speaks and “every mouth may be stopped” (3:19). Each turn names a different person, and each names a different witness.'))
A(('p', 'The study reads only Romans 1:18–3:20. It stops at 3:20 because 3:21 begins with “But now.” Study 54 (Justification) and Study 43 (The Gospel of the Grace of God) read what follows; Study 59 (The Law’s Jurisdiction and the Body of Christ) reads the law’s place; Study 35 (Who Is the Israel of God?) reads the identity question at Romans 2:28–29 as a label. This study asks one narrower question: what does Paul charge against whom in these verses, what witness does he name, and where does the charge stop?'))
A(('p', 'Two habits govern the work. First, each passage is read in its own place, with its speaker, its audience and the words that bind its sentences. Second, a link between passages is made only where a passage makes it. This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting. Where the text is silent, the study records the silence and stops.'))

A(('h1', S1))
A(('p', '1.1 The address. The letter is addressed “To all that be in Rome, beloved of God, called to be saints” (1:7). Paul speaks of himself as “a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle, separated unto the gospel of God” (1:1). The passage under study begins eleven verses after the statement “I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ: for it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth; to the Jew first, and also to the Greek” (1:16). Verse 18 opens with “For,” and the study reads it as a continuation of that argument and records no more than the word states.'))
A(('p', '1.2 The turns of address. The passage does not speak to one audience throughout. Verses 18–32 speak of “men” and “they.” Chapter 2 opens with “O man, whosoever thou art that judgest” (2:1). Verse 17 turns to “thou art called a Jew.” Chapter 3 moves between “we” and “they” and closes with “them who are under the law” and “all the world” (3:19). The study marks each turn and does not read one part as if it were addressed to the audience of another.'))
A(('call', 'Who Is Addressed at Each Turn',
   'Before each sentence the study asks who is being spoken of or spoken to: “men” (1:18), “O man” (2:1), “a Jew” (2:17), or “both Jews and Gentiles” (3:9). A charge laid on one is not read as a charge laid on another unless the passage says so.'))
A(('p', '1.3 The words. Four words recur: “wrath,” “judgment,” “law” and “works” or “deeds.” The study notes where each stands and what is set beside it. The word “sin” stands in the singular in 3:9 (“under sin”) and the passage does not say there that every person commits every sin it lists. The study records that and leaves the connection unmade.'))

A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 Verses 18–20. “For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men, who hold the truth in unrighteousness” (1:18). The sentence states what is revealed (wrath), where it is revealed from (heaven) and against what (“all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men”). Verse 19 states that “that which may be known of God is manifest in them; for God hath shewed it unto them,” and verse 20 states that “the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made, even his eternal power and Godhead; so that they are without excuse.”'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Rom 1:18', '“For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men, who hold the truth in unrighteousness;”', 'What is revealed, from where and against what. The verb is in the present: “is revealed.”'),
    ('Rom 1:19–20', '“Because that which may be known of God is manifest in them; for God hath shewed it unto them. For the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made, even his eternal power and Godhead; so that they are without excuse:”', 'What is “manifest in them,” what is “clearly seen,” and the stated result: “without excuse.”'),
    ('Rom 1:21–23', '“Because that, when they knew God, they glorified him not as God, neither were thankful; but became vain in their imaginations, and their foolish heart was darkened. Professing themselves to be wise, they became fools, And changed the glory of the uncorruptible God into an image made like to corruptible man, and to birds, and fourfooted beasts, and creeping things.”', 'What they did when they “knew God,” and the exchange stated in verse 23.'),
    ('Rom 1:24', '“Wherefore God also gave them up to uncleanness through the lusts of their own hearts, to dishonour their own bodies between themselves:”', 'The first “gave them up.” God is the subject of the verb, and “Wherefore” ties it to what precedes.'),
    ('Rom 1:32', '“Who knowing the judgment of God, that they which commit such things are worthy of death, not only do the same, but have pleasure in them that do them.”', 'What is said to be known, and the last charge of the chapter: doing the same and having “pleasure in them that do them.”'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '2.2 What is revealed. Verse 18 is the first sentence of the passage. It says that wrath “is revealed from heaven.” It does not say in the same verse how the revealing is made, to whom, or when. The passage that follows describes people, not the manner of the revealing. The study records the sentence as written and does not add a method.'))
A(('p', '2.3 What is “manifest” and “clearly seen.” Verses 19–20 name two things: “that which may be known of God” and “the invisible things of him,” described as “his eternal power and Godhead.” Both are said to be shown, and the result is that “they are without excuse.” The verses do not say how much any person has understood, and they do not set out a doctrine of what creation teaches. The study stays with the words.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The exchange and the verbs. Verses 21–23 state that “when they knew God, they glorified him not as God, neither were thankful,” and that they “changed the glory of the uncorruptible God into an image.” Then the verbs change. Three times a sentence says God acted: “God also gave them up” (1:24), “God gave them up unto vile affections” (1:26) and “God gave them over to a reprobate mind” (1:28). Each follows a statement of what they had done (1:25, 1:26–27 and 1:28 “even as they did not like to retain God in their knowledge”). The study reports the three sentences and the order and does not explain how God’s act and their act relate.'))
A(('p', '2.5 The list and the close. Verses 29–31 list what they are “filled with,” from “all unrighteousness” to “unmerciful.” The list is long, and verse 32 ends the chapter with a charge about knowing: “Who knowing the judgment of God, that they which commit such things are worthy of death, not only do the same, but have pleasure in them that do them.” The chapter speaks of “they” throughout. It does not name the people by nation or place, and the study does not supply a name.'))

A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', '3.1 Verses 1–5. Chapter 2 turns to a hearer. “Therefore thou art inexcusable, O man, whosoever thou art that judgest: for wherein thou judgest another, thou condemnest thyself; for thou that judgest doest the same things” (2:1). The sentence speaks to “O man” and does not name his nation. Verse 2 states that “the judgment of God is according to truth against them which commit such things,” and verses 3–4 put two questions to him about escaping and about “the riches of his goodness and forbearance and longsuffering.” Verse 4 ends with “the goodness of God leadeth thee to repentance.” Verse 5 states what he “treasurest up unto thyself”: “wrath against the day of wrath and revelation of the righteous judgment of God.”'))
A(('p', '3.2 Verses 6–11. “Who will render to every man according to his deeds” (2:6). The verses that follow set two outcomes side by side. To “them who by patient continuance in well doing seek for glory and honour and immortality” the verse states “eternal life” (2:7). To those who “are contentious, and do not obey the truth, but obey unrighteousness” the verse states “indignation and wrath, Tribulation and anguish” (2:8–9). Both are said to fall “of the Jew first, and also of the Gentile” (2:9–10), and verse 11 closes: “For there is no respect of persons with God.”'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Rom 2:1', '“Therefore thou art inexcusable, O man, whosoever thou art that judgest: for wherein thou judgest another, thou condemnest thyself; for thou that judgest doest the same things.”', 'The address changes from “they” to “thou” and “O man.” The one who judges is charged with doing the same things.'),
    ('Rom 2:6–8', '“Who will render to every man according to his deeds: To them who by patient continuance in well doing seek for glory and honour and immortality, eternal life: But unto them that are contentious, and do not obey the truth, but obey unrighteousness, indignation and wrath,”', 'The rendering is “according to his deeds.” Two groups and two outcomes are named, with “eternal life” at one end and “indignation and wrath” at the other.'),
    ('Rom 2:14–15', '“For when the Gentiles, which have not the law, do by nature the things contained in the law, these, having not the law, are a law unto themselves: Which shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also bearing witness, and their thoughts the mean while accusing or else excusing one another;)”', 'Gentiles “which have not the law,” and what they “shew”: “the work of the law written in their hearts,” “their conscience also bearing witness,” thoughts “accusing or else excusing.”'),
    ('Rom 2:16', '“In the day when God shall judge the secrets of men by Jesus Christ according to my gospel.”', 'A day, what is judged (“the secrets of men”), and the words “by Jesus Christ according to my gospel.”'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '3.3 Verses 12–13. “For as many as have sinned without law shall also perish without law: and as many as have sinned in the law shall be judged by the law” (2:12). Two groups are named, those “without law” and those “in the law,” with a different clause for each. Verse 13, in parentheses, states that “not the hearers of the law are just before God, but the doers of the law shall be justified.” The sentence is a statement about hearers and doers. The study does not set it against Romans 3:20 or 3:28 in this chapter of the study; each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '3.4 Verses 14–15: the Gentiles and conscience. The sentence states that “the Gentiles, which have not the law, do by nature the things contained in the law,” that they are “a law unto themselves,” and that they “shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also bearing witness, and their thoughts the mean while accusing or else excusing one another.” Four things are named: what they do, what they “shew,” a witness (“their conscience”) and thoughts that accuse or excuse. The verse does not say whether these Gentiles are believers or unbelievers, how the witness of conscience is formed, or how reliable it is. The study does not decide any of these and builds no doctrine of conscience from the sentence alone. The word comes again in Section 6.'))
A(('call', 'What Romans 2:6–11 Does and Does Not Say',
   'The verses state a rendering “according to his deeds,” two outcomes and two audiences, “to the Jew first, and also to the Gentile.” The study reports them as written. It does not draw from them a doctrine of how any person is saved or lost, it does not set them against another passage to reconcile them, and it does not carry them to readers who are not addressed in them.'))
A(('p', '3.5 Verse 16. “In the day when God shall judge the secrets of men by Jesus Christ according to my gospel.” The verse names a day, what is judged and the one by whom (“by Jesus Christ”). It adds “according to my gospel.” The study reports the verse as written. It does not name the day by any other title, assign it a place in a sequence of judgments, or say what “my gospel” includes beyond the words.'))

A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Verses 17–24. “Behold, thou art called a Jew, and restest in the law, and makest thy boast of God” (2:17). The address now names a person: “a Jew.” Verses 17–20 list what he rests in and claims (the law, God’s will, being “a guide of the blind”). Verses 21–23 turn the list into questions: “thou therefore which teachest another, teachest thou not thyself?” and “thou that makest thy boast of the law, through breaking the law dishonourest thou God?” Verse 24 adds a written sentence: “For the name of God is blasphemed among the Gentiles through you, as it is written.”'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Rom 2:17–18', '“Behold, thou art called a Jew, and restest in the law, and makest thy boast of God, And knowest his will, and approvest the things that are more excellent, being instructed out of the law;”', 'The address changes to “thou art called a Jew.” What he rests in, boasts of and knows.'),
    ('Rom 2:25', '“For circumcision verily profiteth, if thou keep the law: but if thou be a breaker of the law, thy circumcision is made uncircumcision.”', 'Circumcision “profiteth” on a stated condition, and the condition fails if the law is broken.'),
    ('Rom 2:28–29', '“For he is not a Jew, which is one outwardly; neither is that circumcision, which is outward in the flesh: But he is a Jew, which is one inwardly; and circumcision is that of the heart, in the spirit, and not in the letter; whose praise is not of men, but of God.”', 'Two sentences set “outwardly” beside “inwardly,” and “the letter” beside “the heart.” No group is named as the one meant.'),
    ('Rom 3:1–2', '“What advantage then hath the Jew? or what profit is there of circumcision? Much every way: chiefly, because that unto them were committed the oracles of God.”', 'A question and its answer: “Much every way,” with one reason given, “the oracles of God.”'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '4.2 Verses 25–29: circumcision. “For circumcision verily profiteth, if thou keep the law: but if thou be a breaker of the law, thy circumcision is made uncircumcision” (2:25). Verses 26–27 reverse the picture for “the uncircumcision” that keeps “the righteousness of the law.” Verse 28 states that “he is not a Jew, which is one outwardly,” and verse 29 that “he is a Jew, which is one inwardly; and circumcision is that of the heart, in the spirit, and not in the letter; whose praise is not of men, but of God.” The study reports these sentences as written. It does not turn “a Jew … inwardly” into a label for a group, and it does not set the verse in a theory of identity; Study 35 takes up that question.'))
A(('p', '4.3 Verses 3:1–2. “What advantage then hath the Jew? or what profit is there of circumcision? Much every way: chiefly, because that unto them were committed the oracles of God.” The question follows the turn of chapter 2, and the answer is stated at once: “Much every way.” The one reason given is that “unto them were committed the oracles of God.” The text does not list further advantages in this passage, and the study adds none.'))
A(('p', '4.4 Verses 3:3–8. Five questions and answers follow, among them “shall their unbelief make the faith of God without effect?” answered with “God forbid: yea, let God be true, but every man a liar” (3:3–4) and “how shall God judge the world?” (3:6). Verse 8 refers to a slander: “Let us do evil, that good may come? whose damnation is just.” The study records the questions and the answers as they stand. The passage is argued as a series of objections, and the study does not state their application beyond the verses.'))

A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', '5.1 Verse 9: the question and the charge. “What then? are we better than they? No, in no wise: for we have before proved both Jews and Gentiles, that they are all under sin” (3:9). Two things are stated: the charge is laid on “both Jews and Gentiles,” and the phrase is “all under sin.” The verse refers back (“we have before proved”) to what chapters 1 and 2 argued.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Rom 3:9', '“What then? are we better than they? No, in no wise: for we have before proved both Jews and Gentiles, that they are all under sin;”', 'Both groups named, and the phrase “under sin.” The verse closes with a semicolon, and “As it is written” follows.'),
    ('Rom 3:10–12', '“As it is written, There is none righteous, no, not one: There is none that understandeth, there is none that seeketh after God. They are all gone out of the way, they are together become unprofitable; there is none that doeth good, no, not one.”', 'The first three written sentences. The words “none” and “not one” recur, and the verb of seeking is negated.'),
    ('Rom 3:19', '“Now we know that what things soever the law saith, it saith to them who are under the law: that every mouth may be stopped, and all the world may become guilty before God.”', 'What the law says and to whom (“them who are under the law”), and two results: “every mouth … stopped” and “all the world … guilty before God.”'),
    ('Rom 3:20', '“Therefore by the deeds of the law there shall no flesh be justified in his sight: for by the law is the knowledge of sin.”', 'The last verse of the passage. It begins with “Therefore” and names the law’s relation to “the knowledge of sin.”'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '5.2 The written sentences (3:10–18). The verses are introduced by “As it is written” and run through verse 18: sentences about righteousness (“There is none righteous, no, not one”), understanding and seeking (3:11), going out of the way (3:12), the throat, the tongue and the lips (3:13), the mouth (3:14), the feet (3:15–16), the way of peace (3:17) and the fear of God (3:18). The study records the sentences as the passage gives them. The text does not say in these verses that each sentence describes every individual, and the study does not say so either.'))
A(('call', 'Where the Charge Stops',
   'The charge ends at 3:20. Verse 3:21 begins with “But now,” and what follows is read in other studies. The study does not carry the charge into 3:21 and does not carry the verdict of 3:21–26 back into chapters 1–3.'))
A(('p', '5.3 Verses 19–20. “Now we know that what things soever the law saith, it saith to them who are under the law.” The sentence restricts the audience of the law’s saying to “them who are under the law,” and then states two results: that “every mouth may be stopped” and that “all the world may become guilty before God.” Verse 20 draws the consequence: “by the deeds of the law there shall no flesh be justified in his sight: for by the law is the knowledge of sin.” The study reports the two verses in order. It does not give a doctrine of the law’s place for the Body, and it does not read verse 20 as a statement about faith; the passage leaves that to what follows. Study 59 takes up the law’s jurisdiction.'))

A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', '6.1 One word, several settings. The word “conscience” appears in Romans 2:15 and in other places in Paul’s letters. Each stands in its own sentence, with its own speaker and its own subject, and the study sets them side by side only to record what each says. The table gives six. It does not give every verse, and Study 77 and the commentary volume on conscience take up the verses that belong to their own subjects. First Timothy and Titus take the letter-form line: this reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Rom 9:1', '“I say the truth in Christ, I lie not, my conscience also bearing me witness in the Holy Ghost,”', 'Paul’s own conscience, “bearing me witness,” with “in the Holy Ghost.”'),
    ('Rom 13:5', '“Wherefore ye must needs be subject, not only for wrath, but also for conscience sake.”', 'Conscience as a reason, with “wrath,” for a stated duty. Study 77 reads the passage.'),
    ('1 Cor 8:7', '“Howbeit there is not in every man that knowledge: for some with conscience of the idol unto this hour eat it as a thing offered unto an idol; and their conscience being weak is defiled.”', 'A “weak” conscience that is “defiled.” The setting is food offered to idols.'),
    ('2 Cor 4:2', '“But have renounced the hidden things of dishonesty, not walking in craftiness, nor handling the word of God deceitfully; but by manifestation of the truth commending ourselves to every man’s conscience in the sight of God.”', 'The speakers commend themselves “to every man’s conscience in the sight of God.”'),
    ('1 Tim 1:5', '“Now the end of the commandment is charity out of a pure heart, and of a good conscience, and of faith unfeigned:”', 'A “good conscience” named beside “a pure heart” and “faith unfeigned.”'),
    ('Titus 1:15', '“Unto the pure all things are pure: but unto them that are defiled and unbelieving is nothing pure; but even their mind and conscience is defiled.”', 'Conscience named with “mind,” and “defiled.” The words “unbelieving” and “defiled” stand together.'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '6.2 What the table shows. The word stands in six different settings, and the nouns beside it differ: “witness” (Romans 9:1), “wrath” and “sake” (13:5), “weak” and “defiled” (1 Corinthians 8:7), “commending ourselves” (2 Corinthians 4:2), “good” and “pure” (1 Timothy 1:5) and “defiled” (Titus 1:15). Romans 2:15 names conscience as a witness among “their thoughts … accusing or else excusing.” The study notes the shared word and does not state that the six passages teach one doctrine. It does not say what conscience is made of, how it is formed or whether it is a guide to be trusted by itself. The passages as written do not set that out.'))

A(('call', 'Conscience in Romans 2:15',
   'The verse names a witness, “their conscience also bearing witness,” and thoughts that accuse or excuse. The study reports the sentence as written. It does not say how conscience is formed or whether it can be trusted by itself, and it does not build a doctrine of conscience from one verse.'))

A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The charge side by side. The table sets out the four turns of the passage. It lists what the text states in each and what the text leaves unsaid.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part', 'Whom and with what', 'Witness named', 'What it leaves unsaid'],
    ['1:18–32', '“Men” who “hold the truth in unrighteousness”; the exchange and the list', '“That which may be known of God,” “the invisible things of him”', 'Who is meant by nation or place; how God’s “gave them up” and their act relate'],
    ['2:1–16', '“O man” who judges and does “the same things”; Gentiles “which have not the law”', '“Thou … the same,” “a law unto themselves,” “their conscience also bearing witness”', 'Whether the Gentiles are believers; the manner of the witness; what the “day” is called elsewhere'],
    ['2:17–3:8', '“A Jew” who rests in the law and breaks it', '“The law,” circumcision, “the oracles of God”', 'How “a Jew … inwardly” relates to any other group; further advantages'],
    ['3:9–20', '“Both Jews and Gentiles … all under sin”', '“As it is written,” “what things soever the law saith”', 'Whether each sentence describes every individual; what follows 3:20'],
], [0.12, 0.33, 0.28, 0.27]))
A(('p', '7.2 What the passage leaves unsaid. The second table lists matters the passage does not settle in its own words. Each row sets what the passage states beside what it leaves unsaid.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Matter', 'What the passage states', 'What it leaves unsaid'],
    ['The manner of the revealing', '“the wrath of God is revealed from heaven” (1:18)', 'How and to whom the revealing is made; the time of its fulfilment.'],
    ['What creation shows', '“his eternal power and Godhead” “clearly seen” (1:20)', 'How much any person has understood; a doctrine of natural knowledge.'],
    ['God’s act and theirs', '“God gave them up” (1:24, 26) and “they did not like to retain God” (1:28)', 'How the two relate.'],
    ['The Gentiles of 2:14', '“do by nature the things contained in the law”', 'Whether they are believers; how the witness arises.'],
    ['The rendering of 2:6–10', '“according to his deeds”; “eternal life”; “indignation and wrath”', 'How any person comes to either end; how the verses relate to other passages.'],
    ['“A Jew … inwardly”', '“he is a Jew, which is one inwardly” (2:29)', 'Any group or label; a statement of program.'],
    ['The written sentences', '“As it is written” (3:10)', 'Whether each describes every individual.'],
], [0.24, 0.38, 0.38]))
A(('p', '7.3 Two readings tested. Two readings of the passage are common, and each can be set beside the words. (a) The passage charges every person with every sin it lists. The text states “all under sin” (3:9) and lists sins in 1:29–31 and in the written sentences; it does not state that each person has done each one, and it does not say in these verses how each sentence is distributed. The study records the charge and the lists as written. (b) Conscience is a full guide to what is right. The text states that conscience bears witness and that thoughts accuse or excuse (2:15); elsewhere the same word stands beside “weak” and “defiled” (1 Corinthians 8:7; Titus 1:15). The study sets the verses beside one another and states no more than they do.'))

A(('h1', 'Conclusion'))
A(('p', 'Romans 1:18–3:20 charges in order and by turns. It speaks of “men” who hold the truth in unrighteousness, of “O man” who judges, of “a Jew” who rests in the law, and of “both Jews and Gentiles … all under sin,” and it ends where “every mouth may be stopped.” It names a witness at each turn: what may be known of God, a law “written in their hearts” with conscience bearing witness, the oracles of God and the law. The study has recorded what each part states, to whom, and where it stops.'))

A(('h1', 'Study Summary'))
A(('bul', [
    'Romans 1:18–32 states wrath revealed “from heaven,” what is “manifest” and “clearly seen,” the exchange of verses 21–23 and three sentences in which God “gave them up” or “over” (1:24, 26, 28).',
    'Romans 2:1–16 speaks to “O man” who judges, states a rendering “according to his deeds,” and names Gentiles who “shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also bearing witness.”',
    'Romans 2:17–3:8 speaks to “a Jew,” states that circumcision “profiteth” if the law is kept, states “Much every way: chiefly, because that unto them were committed the oracles of God,” and answers objections.',
    'Romans 3:9–20 states “both Jews and Gentiles … all under sin,” quotes written sentences, and ends with “every mouth … stopped” and “all the world … guilty before God.”',
    'Conscience appears in six settings in Paul’s letters; the word is shared and each setting is its own.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Key Distinctions to Retain'))
A(('bul', [
    'Who is addressed matters: “men,” “O man,” “a Jew,” and “both Jews and Gentiles” are four turns, and a charge laid on one is not read as laid on another unless the text does so.',
    'Where the passage states a witness (what may be known of God, a law written in the heart, conscience, the oracles, the law), the study records it and does not build a doctrine of conscience from one sentence.',
    'The charge ends at Romans 3:20; what follows from 3:21 is not read into it, and the charge is not read into the verses after it.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Scripture-Tracing Exercise'))
A(('p', 'Trace the passages in the order below. For each step, read the verses in their chapter, write what the text states, and write what it leaves unsaid.'))
A(('num', [
    'Read Romans 1:7 and 1:16–18 and write the address and the sentence on which verse 18 rests.',
    'Read Romans 1:18–20 and write what is revealed, from where, and what is said to be “manifest” and “clearly seen.”',
    'Read Romans 1:21–32 and write the three sentences in which God “gave them up” or “over” and what each follows.',
    'Read Romans 2:1–11 and write who is addressed, what is stated of judgment and the two outcomes of verses 7–9.',
    'Read Romans 2:12–16 and write the two groups of verse 12, the Gentiles of verse 14 and the four things named in verses 14–15.',
    'Read Romans 2:17–29 and write what “a Jew” rests in, what is charged, and what verses 28–29 state without adding a label.',
    'Read Romans 3:1–9 and write the advantage named in 3:2 and the phrase of verse 9.',
    'Read Romans 3:10–20 and write the written sentences, the audience of the law in 3:19 and where the passage stops.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Review and Discussion Questions'))
A(('num', [
    'Who is addressed in Romans 1:18, 2:1, 2:17 and 3:9, and what does each turn charge?',
    'What does Romans 2:15 name as a witness, and what does the passage leave unsaid about it?',
    'What does Romans 3:2 give as the one advantage named in that passage?',
    'Where does the passage state that “all the world” is guilty, and to whom does the verse say the law speaks?',
    'Where does the study stop, and what word opens the verse after it?',
]))

A(('h1', 'Teaching Outline'))
A(('p', 'I. The setting. A. The address (Romans 1:7, 1:16–18). B. The turns of address. C. The four recurring words.'))
A(('p', 'II. Romans 1:18–32. A. What is revealed. B. “Manifest” and “clearly seen.” C. The exchange. D. “Gave them up.” E. The list and the close.'))
A(('p', 'III. Romans 2:1–16. A. The one who judges. B. The rendering “according to his deeds.” C. The Gentiles and conscience. D. The day (2:16).'))
A(('p', 'IV. Romans 2:17–3:8. A. “A Jew.” B. Circumcision. C. The oracles of God. D. The objections.'))
A(('p', 'V. Romans 3:9–20. A. “All under sin.” B. The written sentences. C. The law’s audience. D. The close at 3:20.'))
A(('p', 'VI. Conscience in Paul’s letters. A. Six settings. B. What the word does and does not show.'))

A(('h1', 'Guided Further Study'))
A(('p', 'Read each group in context, observe the point named and write one sentence of conclusion.'))
A(('p', 'Group 1: The turns of address. Read Romans 1:18, 2:1, 2:17 and 3:9. Observe the word that addresses each audience. Conclude who is charged in each.'))
A(('p', 'Group 2: The witnesses. Read Romans 1:19–20, 2:14–15, 3:2 and 3:19. Observe what each names as a witness. Conclude what the text states and what it leaves to the reader.'))
A(('p', 'Group 3: The written sentences. Read Romans 3:10–18 with Psalm 14:1–3, Psalm 5:9 and Isaiah 59:7–8 in their own settings. Observe how Paul introduces them. Conclude what the text states of each.'))

A(('h1', 'Final Synthesis'))
A(('p', 'In about 250 words, state what Romans 1:18–3:20 says about the charge laid on the world, show whom each turn addresses, set the passage’s witnesses side by side and record what it leaves unsaid, quoting the passage.'))
